From 108897f28e93d75d1477d56882771814a03740e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 23:46:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(routing): WAL routing state; sticky reuse
 prepares outside the writer lock
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem: 2026-11-02 21:23-21:30 UTC on hermes-hub every model-routing allocation failed with
"database is locked": managed workers at model_routing_state.py:32 (COMMIT) and :20 (CREATE TABLE
IF NOT EXISTS), gateway context compression, delegate_task provider resolution and tui agent
builds (managed-worker.log, agent.log, errors.log). Worker run 28218 died at startup.

Why 1: allocators' COMMIT waited 30 s for an EXCLUSIVE lock and timed out — evidence: the
  21:26:37 gateway control-loop stack dump shows a thread parked in allocate() at line 32.
Why 2: a COMMIT in the rollback journal needs every SHARED reader gone, and a writer waiting at
  COMMIT keeps PENDING, which refuses every new reader (schema check, peek, sticky lookup) —
  evidence: isolated repro on sqlite 3.53.1 (journal_mode=delete: held BEGIN+SELECT makes COMMIT,
  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, SELECT and a mode=ro peek all return "database is locked"; WAL: all ok).
Why 3: one read transaction was held for 8.5 min — evidence: maintenance dry run
  `hydrate_allocations.py --routes ~/.hermes/model-routing.sqlite3` (card t_01a19d6f5cf8f953)
  ran 21:21:38-21:30:15 (`/usr/bin/time` Elapsed 8:36.92), opening `BEGIN` with the routing DB
  ATTACHed and streaming every table; the error window ends with it (last error 21:30:00).
Why 4: model-routing.sqlite3 was created in the default rollback journal (live file: `delete`),
  so any reader can stall all writers and, through PENDING, all other readers.
Contributing: every per-turn sticky re-resolution (model_tools._resolve_active_context_length ->
  resolve_runtime_provider -> allocate) took BEGIN IMMEDIATE and held it across prepare()
  (credential-pool select, possibly an auth-store flock wait and token refresh), queueing all live
  agents behind any slow first allocation.
Root (happened): a shared multi-process state DB used the rollback journal and took the writer
  lock for reads.
Root (not caught): routing tests never held a concurrent reader or a slow prepare.

Fix:
- Writer connections switch the file to WAL (once; a conversion blocked by a reader is retried by
  the next connection within a 1 s budget instead of failing allocation). Readers never block a
  commit, and a commit never blocks readers or the read-only peek.
- A sticky reuse that writes nothing is read from a snapshot and prepared outside the writer lock.
  Every write (first allocation, alias, failover, exact-pin upgrade, replace) still prepares
  before COMMIT, so an unready credential/client is never published; counters only move under the
  lock. One `_reusable` rule serves both paths.
- Schema DDL (now including switch_fences) stays autocommit before BEGIN IMMEDIATE, so a
  rolled-back first allocation keeps the tables for peek/link_continuation; on an existing schema
  in WAL it only reads.
- No whole-transaction retry: prepare has credential-pool side effects (account selection,
  refresh). SQLite's busy handler (BUSY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30) stays the single bounded retry, before
  any allocation work. A writer-lock hold >= 5 s logs a warning.

Tests (tests/agent/test_model_routing_state_contention.py, real files and connections, processes
for the allocator fan-out): long reader vs new allocation; peek/schema/sticky reads with a reader
plus a waiting writer; slow prepare vs sticky reuse, peek and a queued first allocation; busy
deadline exhaustion (bounded, nothing prepared or published); 4-process allocators under a long
reader (60 unique aliases, counter 60, exact 20/20/20 rotation); same-session concurrent
allocators share one decision; sticky snapshot never moves counters and writes still lock;
legacy rollback file conversion; conversion blocked by a reader (fallback asserted); ro peek
with/without sidecars; rejected first allocation keeps the schema (review finding on a draft).
RED on the parent: 7 of 10 fail ("database is locked", 30 s deadline; the rest are invariant
controls); GREEN: 11/11. Routing suite (42 files, 377 tests incl. switch-intent fence/order) green.
